# 5. VR CAPABILITY DETECTION AND SESSION PREPARATION

The workspace must distinguish genuine immersive VR from non-immersive 3D review.

Use runtime capability detection. Do not infer VR support from user-agent strings, device labels, desktop width, mobile status, or the mere presence of Three.js.

Where supported by the existing runtime, inspect:

- Secure-context availability.
- Presence of `navigator.xr`.
- Result of `navigator.xr.isSessionSupported('immersive-vr')`.
- WebGL renderer readiness.
- Browser focus and document visibility.
- Permission-Policy restrictions relevant to XR spatial tracking.
- Actual available reference spaces and input sources.
- Renderer or headset session conflicts.
- Model validity and scene-preparation status.

WebXR documentation:
- API overview: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebXR_Device_API
- Permissions and security: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebXR_Device_API/Permissions_and_security
- Session startup and shutdown: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebXR_Device_API/Startup_and_shutdown
- Application lifecycle: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebXR_Device_API/Lifecycle

Do not claim that a successful capability check guarantees that the headset will start, that controllers will be available, or that a large model will run smoothly.

Use clearly defined workspace modes.

## Mode A — Immersive VR Review

Enter this mode only when actual immersive VR session support has been confirmed and session startup succeeds.

Display the available navigation methods and controls based on the actual detected input sources. Do not advertise hand tracking, eye tracking, controller haptics, or advanced headset features unless they are genuinely supported and implemented.

## Mode B — Standard 3D Design Review

Provide a functional non-immersive Three.js review when the browser or device cannot start immersive VR.

Support appropriate orbit, pan, zoom, camera presets, model inspection, layer filtering, and available annotation actions.

The fallback must not depend on successfully starting an XR session.

## Mode C — Review Setup and Calibration

Provide a non-immersive workspace for checking model scale, orientation, review visibility, navigation settings, and annotation settings before entering VR.

This mode must never be labelled an immersive VR session.

If VR is unsupported, the UI should remain useful and explain why immersive entry is unavailable.

# 6. SAFE SESSION START, LIFECYCLE, AND EXIT

Follow the current WebXR and Three.js lifecycle patterns.

Use the actual renderer's XR manager where appropriate. In Three.js, inspect the current APIs and versions before implementation; `WebXRManager` documentation is available at:

https://threejs.org/docs/pages/WebXRManager.html

Requirements:

1. Start an immersive session only in response to an intentional user action.
2. Confirm the current document and runtime can support the request.
3. Handle rejected session requests without crashing the workspace.
4. Detect session start, visibility change, end, and unexpected interruption.
5. Restore the standard 3D renderer state when the session ends.
6. Remove event listeners and dispose of temporary XR resources.
7. Avoid duplicate sessions or competing render loops.
8. Ensure an interrupted session does not leave the application stuck in a misleading “VR active” state.
9. Provide a clear exit action outside as well as within the appropriate available control flow.
10. Dispose of session-specific resources without accidentally destroying shared project data or an active renderer still used by the standard viewer.

Handle browser navigation, component unmount, application errors, headset disconnection, and runtime-initiated session termination.

Never claim a session has started until the session-start outcome actually confirms it.

Do not automatically request microphone, camera, eye-tracking, or environmental permissions unrelated to the VR review.

# 7. MODEL PREPARATION, SCALE, AND COORDINATE INTEGRITY

Use the existing building and site geometry pipeline wherever compatible.

Reuse the real current implementation of `building3dGeometry.ts` and P48's scene/model preparation logic rather than creating parallel implementations of rooms, walls, slabs, roof geometry, terrain, and structural components.

Before displaying a model in VR, validate:

- The project and scene contain supported geometry.
- Dimensions and units are understood.
- Required coordinates and transforms are finite.
- The expected vertical axis and orientation are correct.
- Geometry contains no unsupported or malformed data that would break the viewer.
- The model fits within the current rendering and interaction budget.
- Any unsupported elements are disclosed.

## 7.1 Scale modes

Provide explicit, clearly labelled review scales where supported by the current model data, for example:

- 1:1 — full-scale walkthrough.
- 1:10 — reduced model review.
- 1:20 — studio model.
- 1:50 or 1:100 — compact whole-project review.

Do not offer scales that create an incoherent or unusable review mode for the current model.

Clearly state whether scale is derived from validated geometry or from a user-configured preview transform.

Use one well-defined transformation path. Do not apply the same unit conversion or scale multiplier twice.

Verify metre/foot conversion and orientation with known test fixtures. Preserve the existing coordinate contract.

If the model requires a scale correction because its source units are unknown, ask for an explicit configuration within the UI rather than silently guessing.

## 7.2 Full-scale navigation

In a supported full-scale room walkthrough, the viewer's tracked head and controller positions must drive the virtual perspective according to the XR runtime.

Do not overwrite the tracked XR camera transform with the ordinary desktop camera on every render.

Keep scene placement, floor height, virtual origin, and user height assumptions distinct from any claim of surveyed physical placement.

If the reference space or floor-level assumptions differ across runtimes, expose the applicable calibration or fallback and document its limitation.

P49 is a virtual scene review. It does not inherit geospatial registration from P48, and it must not claim that a VR model is aligned with a real property, GNSS coordinate, surveyed site, or construction setting-out grid unless actual validated registration exists.

# 8. VR NAVIGATION AND COMFORT

Implement the navigation modes that can be supported reliably by the actual runtime.

Prefer comfort-oriented options over unnecessary realism.

## Required interaction options

Where supported:

- Controller-directed selection and pointing.
- Controller ray interaction with reviewable objects.
- Direct selection or gaze selection only when the runtime exposes an appropriate input method.
- Teleportation to valid navigation destinations.
- Snap turning.
- Seated or stationary review.
- Enter/exit control accessible without walking to a distant virtual button.
- Return-to-origin or recenter controls where the runtime supports the intended behaviour.
- A clear distinction between physical headset movement and virtual locomotion.

Do not assume every headset has both controllers or identical buttons. Inspect `XRInputSource` information and adapt the interaction UI to the actual input devices.

Reference:
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebXR_Device_API/Inputs

## Locomotion requirements

1. Offer teleportation or another well-tested low-motion alternative wherever supported.
2. Provide configurable snap-turn increments where practical.
3. Do not force continuous artificial acceleration as the only navigation method.
4. Avoid unnecessary camera bob, automatic movement, rapid scripted camera transitions, and sudden scene jumps.
5. Keep the user informed before switching scale, origin, or review mode.
6. Provide an immediate exit path.
7. Do not move the virtual view unexpectedly when toggling an ordinary overlay.
8. Prevent teleport destinations from being placed inside invalid model geometry where a supported collision or floor query can detect this.
9. If a valid floor or walkable area cannot be established, do not imply that teleport destinations are physically validated.
10. Include seated and stationary interaction paths that do not assume the user can physically walk through a room.

Provide brief, non-alarming comfort guidance. Do not present a comfort preset as a guarantee that motion discomfort cannot occur.

Do not bypass or disable the headset's own boundary or guardian system.

# 9. ARCHITECTURAL EXPLORATION WORKFLOW

Build a review experience rather than merely dropping the current scene into a headset.

Provide useful navigation and inspection actions, depending on actual model completeness:

- Enter a room or space.
- Return to a whole-building overview.
- Inspect a floor or selected area.
- Inspect available facade, roof, structural, or site components.
- Move between supported floor levels.
- Hide or show selected review layers.
- Focus or isolate a supported component.
- Return to the default review viewpoint.
- Inspect a selected element's available metadata.
- Compare the selected review area with its source model data.
- Open the issue/annotation panel.

Do not generate fake rooms, doors, windows, materials, or building components to create an impression of a complete model.

If the model is missing a roof, upper floor, opening, interior finish, or other element, represent the missing data accurately.

If a layer is a semantic visualization only, clearly distinguish it from a separately modelled physical component.

Avoid overwhelming the user with all site, engineering, carbon, hazard, and annotation layers enabled by default.

Use a clear visual hierarchy. The base model should remain legible, and the user should be able to identify which discipline or source produced an overlay.

# 10. CONTROLLER INTERACTIONS AND SPATIAL SELECTION

Create a predictable interaction system.

Where controller ray selection is supported:

1. Show a visible pointer or targeting indicator.
2. Determine which supported object, surface, or annotation is targeted.
3. Display a clear selected-state indicator.
4. Open relevant inspection information.
5. Permit supported annotation creation or editing.
6. Provide a clear way to cancel or return to navigation.
7. Prevent one trigger action from accidentally performing multiple unrelated operations.
8. Distinguish selection from an action that changes the project.

Do not let selecting a model component automatically edit the underlying project.

Do not confuse controller-ray intersections against the virtual scene with hit testing against the real physical environment. P48's AR placement and P49's virtual object selection are different operations.

If controller input is missing, expose an honest explanation and use another implemented interaction method only if the runtime supplies it.

If no usable interaction method is available, keep the standard 3D fallback accessible.

# 11. REVIEW ANNOTATIONS, ISSUE MARKERS, AND BOOKMARKS

P49 should help users record what they noticed while inspecting a design.

Implement a compact, useful review record with fields or equivalent concepts such as:

- Annotation ID.
- Project ID.
- Review-session ID.
- Target element ID, where available.
- Target room or spatial identifier, where available.
- Local model-space anchor or transform, when supported.
- Annotation title and note.
- Category or issue type.
- User-selected priority.
- Creation/update timestamps.
- Source model or geometry fingerprint.
- Annotation status, if supported by the existing workflow.
- Provenance and data-completeness information.

Potential categories include:

- Spatial layout observation.
- Circulation or wayfinding concern.
- Design coordination issue.
- Missing or unclear model information.
- Visual review note.
- Follow-up inspection required.

Do not present a user's priority selection as a formally assessed engineering risk.

## Annotation placement

Anchor annotations to a stable model-space reference or a stable supported element identifier when possible.

Do not save the annotation only as a raw headset pose that becomes meaningless after the user exits VR.

If a target object is removed, moved, or regenerated, detect whether the annotation can still be resolved. If not, mark it unresolved or needing review rather than quietly attaching it to an unrelated object.

Preserve the model coordinate contract and apply unit conversions consistently.

## Annotation display

Provide a readable annotation panel in standard 3D mode and a suitable VR presentation where technically supported.

Do not place long text directly in the viewer such that it obstructs the model or requires uncomfortable head movement.

Support selection, dismissal, editing, deletion, and appropriate persistence according to established project conventions.

Confirm destructive removal of saved notes where needed.

## Scope boundary

These are P49 review notes. Do not build a full decision-history platform, a design approval engine, or live multi-user collaboration.

