## P44 — Embodied & Whole-Life Carbon Assessment

Show a read-only summary or selected-element details only where the P44 contract supports the relationship.

Do not invent a spatial carbon heatmap by assigning aggregate project totals to arbitrary room surfaces.

Do not recalculate carbon inside P49 or silently change P44 assessment state.

Preserve warnings about missing modules, incomplete quantities, source limitations, and assessment freshness.

## P45 — Climate Hazard & Resilience Analyzer

Show supported assessment summaries and hazard layers only when their spatial applicability, coordinate reference, scale, and input data are appropriate.

If a hazard assessment exists only as a project-level summary, display it as a summary panel rather than fabricating a spatially aligned hazard surface.

Do not turn a conceptual hazard visualization into a claim of parcel-level accuracy or certified risk assessment.

## P46 — Building Envelope Performance Analyzer

Reuse available facade or envelope assessment metadata through the real current P46 contract.

Make it possible to distinguish a semantic highlight from physical envelope thickness or geometry.

Use an explicit legend for existing assessment states such as optimal, moderate, or high risk only if these states actually occur in the source contract.

Do not infer temperatures, heat flow, moisture movement, or U-values from a visual colour.

Show the underlying assessment's data freshness and limitations where available.

## P47 — Construction Site Logistics Planner

Where the existing 3D geometry and coordinate mappings support it, expose relevant site logistics overlays, vehicle paths, zones, crane footprints, and swing-radius visualizations as review layers.

These are design-coordination aids. They do not substitute for a validated lift plan, equipment capacity assessment, verified construction method statement, or site safety approval.

Do not label a crane movement path safe simply because the VR scene contains it.

## P48 — Augmented Reality Site Visualization

Reuse relevant model preparation, geometry builders, scene conventions, transform utilities, and metadata contracts from P48 wherever suitable.

P49 must remain a VR review module, not a second implementation of AR.

P49 must not change P48's capabilities, default placement, session behaviour, or project data merely to obtain a convenient VR view.

Where common code cannot safely be reused without affecting P48, isolate the minimal P49 integration and document its purpose.

The P48 `isGeospatiallyRegistered: false` state must not be silently changed or bypassed. P49 must not imply physical-world alignment just because a model can be viewed at full scale in VR.

# 13. SCENE TRANSFORMS, LAYER FRESHNESS, AND VISUAL CLARITY

Define a predictable scene-assembly process:

1. Load the valid project and source model data.
2. Validate inputs and identify unsupported geometry.
3. Normalize units through one documented transformation path.
4. Build or reuse the model using the established geometry pipeline.
5. Apply the intended review scale and origin.
6. Add selected overlays as separate, identifiable scene groups.
7. Record relevant input fingerprints and source versions.
8. Initialize interaction targets.
9. Start immersive rendering only after the preflight succeeds.
10. Tear down temporary resources correctly.

Do not apply scale to some source groups and forget others. Confirm that relevant layers align with the same coordinate system.

When a source changes, determine which overlays and annotations depend on it.

Show stale results or missing layers explicitly. Do not continue displaying a stale source as current verified data.

Provide a way to hide, reset, or isolate overlay groups.

Use deliberate visual conventions for:

- Base geometry.
- Selected elements.
- Review annotations.
- Terrain.
- P44 assessment summaries, where spatially meaningful.
- P45 hazard information, where spatially meaningful.
- P46 envelope highlights.
- P47 logistics information.

Do not rely on colour alone to communicate a status. Use labels, icons, legends, or patterns where practical.

# 14. PERFORMANCE AND RESOURCE MANAGEMENT

VR requires a responsive rendering loop. A build that succeeds on a desktop browser does not prove that the model will run adequately inside a headset.

Inspect scene size, triangle count, material count, texture usage, repeated objects, geometry duplication, shadows, draw calls, and overlay cost.

Implement pragmatic safeguards:

- Avoid reconstructing the entire model every render.
- Reuse existing geometry and material resources where ownership allows.
- Avoid duplicate high-resolution meshes for decorative overlays.
- Use sensible scene complexity limits or warnings.
- Make expensive layers optional.
- Avoid unnecessary real-time shadows or post-processing effects in immersive mode.
- Use suitable pixel-ratio and rendering-quality choices according to the existing renderer and supported runtime.
- Reduce unnecessary computation during each XR frame.
- Avoid synchronous, expensive geometry generation in the active XR frame loop.
- Dispose of temporary geometry, materials, textures, and subscriptions correctly.
- Avoid disposing of shared resources still used by another active viewer.
- Handle memory pressure or unsupported scene complexity with a fallback where possible.
- Restore prior renderer settings after leaving immersive mode.
- Keep model loading, preparation, rendering, and session startup errors distinguishable.

Do not claim a specific headset frame rate unless it was measured on that hardware.

If device performance cannot be tested in the available build environment, record that limitation in the implementation report.

# 15. PERSISTENCE, PROJECT SAFETY, AND ROLLBACK

Follow the actual project persistence and versioning conventions.

Do not invent an independent global persistence store if the project already supports optional project-scoped properties.

Persist only the information required for review and recovery, such as:

- Saved annotations.
- Review bookmarks and relevant view configuration.
- Supported annotation targets or spatial anchors.
- Appropriate source model fingerprints.
- Relevant metadata and schema versions.

Do not persist live headset tracking frames, raw sensory streams, temporary XR objects, or a continuously changing headset pose as project history.

Before applying a project modification, create a checkpoint using the existing approved snapshot/version workflow.

P49 must default to read-only inspection. Ordinary viewing, layer switching, selecting elements, and writing a review note must not silently rewrite source architecture, material, terrain, envelope, carbon, or logistics data.

If a future supported operation changes the project, use a preview-and-apply workflow and preserve rollback.

Ensure:

1. Existing projects without P49 data load normally.
2. A new project receives a safe empty review state.
3. Saved notes survive ordinary reloads.
4. Annotation targets are revalidated against the current model.
5. Stale model fingerprints make dependent anchors or results visibly stale.
6. Malformed review data cannot corrupt unrelated project content.
7. Removing a review record preserves the rest of the project.
8. Session exit does not clear valid saved notes.
9. Checkpoints and rollback behave consistently with existing project services.
10. Persistence failures are surfaced honestly.

Use stable object IDs and normalized model-space anchors where available. Avoid relying solely on transient Three.js object references or array indexes.

# 16. PRIVACY AND SECURITY

P49 must not collect data merely because the XR runtime exposes it.

Do not add:

- Camera capture for P49.
- Continuous microphone recording.
- Eye-tracking collection.
- Biometric or behavioural analytics.
- Head-position telemetry uploads.
- Unnecessary external data transmission.
- Automatic sharing of review notes.

Use only XR inputs needed for the implemented interaction flow.

Keep user review notes and project data within the existing application storage and permission model unless an already-authorized workflow explicitly requires otherwise.

Validate user-entered annotations and imported review data. Avoid injecting raw content into unsafe HTML.

Do not log sensitive project notes or full project contents unnecessarily.

If the runtime or application does not permit immersive mode, explain the actual blocker and retain the standard 3D fallback.

