# 18. TEST ARCHITECTURE

Inspect the current test infrastructure and add a dedicated P49 verification suite using the actual runner conventions.

Use realistic fixtures and explicit assertions. Do not rely on tests that merely check whether a function exists or an object is returned.

## Group A — Capability detection

Test:

- `navigator.xr` unavailable.
- `isSessionSupported` returns false.
- `isSessionSupported` rejects.
- Secure context unavailable.
- WebGL renderer unavailable or failed.
- Immersive VR supported.
- Required security policy or focus restrictions prevent startup.
- UI correctly offers standard 3D fallback.
- Feature availability is not inferred solely from a user-agent string.

## Group B — Session lifecycle

Test:

- Session startup occurs only after explicit user action.
- Successful session start.
- Startup failure.
- Runtime interruption.
- Visibility and session-end events.
- User exits through the supported control.
- Browser navigation or component unmount.
- Renderer restoration.
- No duplicate animation loops.
- No duplicated or leaked event listeners.
- No stale “VR active” state after a session ends.

Use mocked WebXR APIs where the test environment cannot provide a physical runtime.

## Group C — Model geometry and coordinates

Test:

- Valid model loading.
- Empty or invalid model.
- Known building dimensions.
- Feet/metres normalization.
- Scale multipliers applied exactly once.
- Model orientation and vertical axis.
- Valid and invalid floor-level assumptions.
- Missing geometry warnings.
- Unsupported CAD-only references remain unrendered as fabricated 3D objects.
- Valid terrain and overlays align to the supported model coordinate convention.

Include known expected numerical values, not only non-null checks.

## Group D — Navigation and controller interactions

Test:

- Available controller input sources.
- Controller ray-target resolution.
- Selection versus annotation actions.
- Unsupported controller/input availability.
- Valid and invalid teleport destinations where the implementation supports floor/collision checking.
- Snap-turn configuration.
- Seated/stationary navigation path.
- Cancellation and reset controls.
- Invalid target does not trigger a mutation.
- Interaction listeners are removed after exit.

## Group E — Annotations and bookmarks

Test:

- Create, read, update, and delete an annotation.
- Annotation persistence across reload.
- Target element or spatial anchor resolution.
- Model changes that invalidate or stale an anchor.
- Removed elements do not silently reassign annotations to unrelated components.
- Invalid annotation input.
- Review notes remain separate from the authoritative model.
- No false claim of professional risk verification.

## Group F — P41–P48 coexistence

Test each actual supported integration:

- P41 element identifiers and source metadata.
- P42 2D-only entities not fabricated into 3D geometry.
- P43 terrain units and mesh alignment.
- P44 assessment summary and missing-data state.
- P45 source or geospatial uncertainty preserved.
- P46 overlay state and assessment freshness.
- P47 logistics geometry and phase filtering where supported.
- P48 scene/transform reuse without changing AR behaviour.
- Absent optional module data does not crash P49.
- One overlay's visibility change does not accidentally hide or mutate unrelated model layers.

## Group G — Persistence and state safety

Test:

- Safe default for a project with no P49 property.
- Legacy project compatibility.
- Review-state persistence.
- Fingerprint and stale-state handling.
- Snapshot before any supported project mutation.
- Failed mutation preserves original data.
- Rollback when the applicable workflow supports a mutation.
